# LF in the working tree, on every platform. This changes nothing that git
# stores -- blobs are already LF -- only what lands on disk at checkout.
#
# core.autocrlf=true on Windows checks text files out with CRLF, and this repo
# compares generated output against committed artifacts BYTE FOR BYTE. A CRLF
# checkout makes those comparisons fail on files whose visible content is
# identical: scripts/regression-gate.sh reported "THE FEED CHANGED, not the
# book" over a diff in which both sides printed the same two hashes, and
# paper-figures.sh called four SVGs stale for the same reason. Neither had
# changed.
#
# It is worse than a false alarm for shell scripts: bash dies on the carriage
# returns outright, and scripts/make-boot-kit.sh packages run-12-8.sh onto a USB
# stick that boots a bare-metal machine, where there is no editor to fix it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so never examined for line endings at all.
*.trace binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.png binary
*.pdf binary
